insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('e4c40f42-2c02-513c-9bfa-7f3af91ec663'::uuid,'d7190e43-31f8-55d7-b646-04574073dedf'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'**CHAPTER 5**\n\nRECOVERY, REHABILITATION AND BUILD BACK BETTER\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, operational, technical and legal validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 5.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 5                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Chapter title**          | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Fifth of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 4 (Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response) and precedes Chapter 6 (DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability).                                                            |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional and legal validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head / Research and Planning focal person for recovery, with the City Planning and Development Office (CPDO) as proposed technical lead for recovery planning                                                                                                                                             |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Legal Office; CPDO; City Engineering Office and Building Official; CSWDO; City Health Office; PESO; City Agriculture Office; City Environment and Natural Resources Office; City Budget Office; City Accountant; Business Permits and Licensing Office; OCD Region VIII; DEPDev Region VIII (formerly NEDA) |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually; after every event that triggers a PDNA or a rehabilitation and recovery program; after a material legal, policy or plan change                                                                                                                                                                |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 5.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft under the adopted ten-chapter structure. Applies the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020) to Ormoc; reconciles the A9 rows and the related A8.7, A10.6 and Part C rows with the controlled IAT v1.7 workbook and the Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2; continues from Chapter 4 SOP 4-09 and form ER-F05; eight SOPs with control blocks; six figures; APA captions; Annexes 5-A to 5-E. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nChapter 4 ends when the activated EOC stands down. SOP 4-09 closes the EOC in a controlled manner, and its transition register (form ER-F05) lists what is still open: displaced families who cannot yet go home, damaged houses and lifelines, lost incomes, unresolved protection cases, and recovery recommendations. Chapter 5 receives that register. It sets out how the City turns the open items into an approved, funded and monitored program that restores living conditions and leaves Ormoc less exposed than before the event.\n\nRecovery is the longest and most expensive phase of a disaster. It is also the phase in which the City has the most room to reduce future risk: houses can be rebuilt outside hazard zones, bridges can be rebuilt to a higher standard, and livelihoods can be diversified. The Philippine DRRM system names this phase "rehabilitation and recovery", one of its four thematic areas, and sets "Build Back Better" as its guiding principle (NEDA, 2020). Build Back Better means that reconstruction restores pre-disaster conditions and also raises resilience standards and includes climate adaptation and mitigation measures.\n\nOrmoc knows the cost of recovery from experience. The 1991 flash flood (Typhoon Uring) remains the deadliest event in the City''s record. Super Typhoon Yolanda (2013), the magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake (2017), Typhoon Ursula (2019) and Typhoon Odette (2021) each left damage that took months or years to repair (Table 5.3). The City has carried out a PDNA before (the 2017 earthquake PDNA cited in the DRRM 10-Year Plan) and runs a cash-for-work program after disasters. What it lacks is a standing system: a pre-disaster recovery framework, a trained PDNA team, a decision route that is written down, and a way to track recovery projects to completion.\n\nThis chapter applies the same systems-thinking and Total Quality Management (TQM) approach as Chapters 1 to 4. Recovery is treated as a cycle with feedback: plan before the event; assess after it; program, fund and build; track to completion; evaluate; and feed the lessons into the land use plan, the contingency plans and the LDRRMP. A program document is not evidence of recovery. Recovery is complete only when the planned outputs are delivered, the intended outcomes are checked, and the residual risk is lower than before.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. Draft 1.0 copies the A9 rows and the related A8.7, A10.6, A3.4 and Part C rows from the controlled IAT v1.7 workbook (reference/Ormoc_CDRRMO-EOC_Institutional_Assessment_Tool_v1-7.xlsx, 12 November 2026) without change. Priority bands were computed from the workbook formula ((4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory, × 1.0 for Good Practice and Part C). All rows are "Pending validation" in the workbook, so the scores remain provisional until the December 2026 validation workshop. The "Feeds Manual Chapter" column of the workbook still reads "MOO Ch.8" for the A9 rows; under the adopted structure these rows feed Chapter 5 (see the Validation and Source Control Register).\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n**Table 5.3**\n\n*Most Significant Disasters Recorded in the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan*\n\n| **Year and event**                   | **Dead** | **Missing**     | **Injured** | **Estimated cost of damage (PHP) and source**                                                                                  |\n|--------------------------------------|----------|-----------------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1991: Typhoon Uring (flash flood)    | 4,922    | More than 3,000 | 3,020       | 604,000,000.00                                                                                                                 |\n| 2013: Super Typhoon Yolanda          | 38       | 0               | 5,089       | Figure printed in the plan is not plausible and must be verified before use (see note). Source: Ormoc City DRRM Plan 2016–2020 |\n| 2017: Magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake | 2        | 0               | 329         | 330,764,454.02. Source: PDNA report ("Paglaum")                                                                                |\n| 2017: Tropical Storm Urduja          | 3        | 0               | 0           | 187,898,674.48. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n| 2019: Typhoon Ursula                 | 0        | 0               | 59          | 159,198,509.59. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n| 2021: Typhoon Odette                 | 0        | 0               | 3           | 159,676,397.89. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* Figures are copied from Table 3 of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (City Government of Ormoc, 2023). The plan prints the Yolanda damage as "Php 8,000,000,000,000.00", which is not plausible for one city and is probably a typographical error; the CDRRMO should confirm the correct figure from the source plan before it is cited. Ordered by year.\n\nThe assessment records five A9 indicators for rehabilitation and recovery. Figure 5.1 shows their scores. The domain index is 1.38 (score × weight summed over the five rows, divided by the total weight of 8), which falls in the Initial band (0.5–1.49). The one Mandatory row, A9.2, scores 2, so Mandatory compliance in the domain is 0 of 1. A9.2 was lowered from 4 to 2 in v1.7 because its only evidence was FGD self-scoring; the score can be restored at validation once the rehabilitation plans and implementation reports are attached.\n\n**Figure 5.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Domain A9, Rehabilitation and Recovery*\n\n![Figure 1: 1.1 What the Records Show](/library/chp5v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores are copied from the A_CDRRMO sheet of IAT v1.7. M = Mandatory; GP = Good Practice; w = weight. The orange bar is the only Mandatory indicator. The domain index is (1×2 + 2×2 + 1×1 + 2×1 + 1×2) ÷ 8 = 1.375, banded on the unrounded value. All rows are pending validation.\n\n**Table 5.4**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                           | **Score** | **Priority** | **Evidence observed and gap (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                                                               |\n|--------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A9.1   | PDNA capacity: trained team and templates ready (GP, w2)                                                           | 1         | Medium       | No trained PDNA team. The 10-Year Plan plans five PDNA positions (2025).                                                                                                                   |\n| A9.2   | Rehabilitation and recovery plan after major events, with build-back-better and resettlement provisions (M, w2)    | 2         | Medium       | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2 (Methodology §5.2). Rehabilitation plans and implementation reports are to be validated.                                                                  |\n| A9.3   | Recovery data (damage, losses, livelihood, housing) tracked to completion (GP, w1)                                 | 1         | Low          | PDNA and RDANA reports exist, but there is no recovery monitoring matrix.                                                                                                                  |\n| A9.4   | Psychosocial and livelihood recovery coordinated with CSWDO, CHO, PESO (GP, w1)                                    | 2         | Low          | A cash-for-work program exists; referral pathways and MOAs were not sighted.                                                                                                               |\n| A9.5   | Pre-disaster recovery framework: roles, PDNA activation, temporary shelter, debris, recovery fund sources (GP, w2) | 1         | Medium       | No framework. The 10-Year Plan and CDP 2024–2030 (PPA 4.1) plan a TWG for the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Plan and PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training; no TWG order was sighted. |\n| A8.7   | MDM and RDANA teams designated and trained (M, w2)                                                                 | 2         | Medium       | MDM protocol covers COVID-19 only (CDRRMC Res. 06 s. 2021); no all-hazards MDM or RDANA team is designated.                                                                                |\n| A10.6  | After-action reviews after significant events, lessons integrated into plans (GP, w2)                              | 2         | Medium       | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2; AAR reports and plan revisions are to be validated.                                                                                                      |\n| A3.4   | PSCP covering CDRRMO and essential city services (M, w2)                                                           | 2         | Medium       | PSCP formulation is planned (CDP PPA 2.5); no PSCP, BIA or essential-functions list in the document set.                                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. A8.7 and A10.6 are shared with Chapters 4 and 7; A3.4 belongs to Chapter 3 and is listed because continuity of City services is the first condition of recovery. Annex 5-A gives the full row set, owners and targets.\n\nThe DRRM 10-Year Plan reaches the same conclusion in its own words. It records that the City has an approved shelter plan and an approved CLUP and Zoning Ordinance but still has to start land development for identified settlement sites; that the cash-for-work program works but there is no loan program for micro, small and medium enterprises; that farmers have limited insurance cover and there is no buffer seed storage; and that RDNA/PDNA teams still need to be organised and their protocols institutionalised (City Government of Ormoc, 2023). The plan already budgets for most of the remedies (Table 5.5).\n\n**Table 5.5**\n\n*Recovery and Rehabilitation PPAs Already Programmed in the DRRM 10-Year Plan*\n\n| **Program, project or activity**          | **Output in the plan**                                                                         | **Office responsible**                                       | **Partner agencies**             |\n|-------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Training of PDNA personnel                | Five PDNA technical personnel hired (2023–2025); additional personnel trained later            | CDRRMO                                                       | OCD; NEDA (now DEPDev)           |\n| Disaster Recovery and Rehabilitation Plan | TWG created; PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training; plan formulated, then revisited and updated  | CDRRMO                                                       | NEDA (now DEPDev); OCD; PHIVOLCS |\n| Buffer seed stocks and storage facilities | Program of works and feasibility study; storage facility built; seeds procured and distributed | City Agriculture Office; City Engineering Office             | Visayas State University         |\n| Lot acquisition for LGU-funded housing    | Lot acquisition through MOA/MOU and legal processes; housing for target beneficiaries          | CDRRMO; CPDO; City Legal Office; OCHO (as named in the plan) | NHA; Pag-IBIG; SHFC              |\n| Capacity building on PDNA                 | PDNA training for CDRRMC and BDRRMC members, then barangay officials                           | CDRRMO                                                       | OCD                              |\n| MSME access to finance                    | Operational LGU-managed MSME loan program                                                      | BPLO                                                         | SB Corp; financial institutions  |\n| Post-disaster waste management            | Clearing and clean-up operations; waste collection vehicle (dump truck with boom)              | ENRO                                                         | —                                |\n\n*Note.* Summarised from Tables 20 and 24 of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Budgets and years are in the plan and its LDRRMF Investment Plan (Table 32); Chapter 6 governs their financing.\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n**Table 5.6**\n\n*Recovery Design Considerations and the Chapter 5 Response*\n\n| **Design consideration**         | **Existing context / evidence**                                                                                                                                                     | **Chapter 5 response**                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Recovery starts before the event | A9.5 scores 1; no pre-disaster recovery framework. The NEDA guide says pre-disaster recovery planning should be done by local DRRMCs.                                               | SOP 5-01 makes the framework a standing, annually updated product of the CDRRMC, adopted as the recovery annex of each contingency plan. |\n| Seamless handover from response  | Chapter 4 SOP 4-09 produces the ER-F05 transition register and the ER-F06 AAR record.                                                                                               | SOP 5-03 receives every ER-F05 item, names its recovery owner and opens the recovery record. Nothing is closed by deactivation alone.    |\n| Assessment capacity              | A8.7 and A9.1: no designated RDANA or PDNA team; five PDNA positions planned.                                                                                                       | SOP 5-02 sets the RDANA-to-PDNA decision route, team composition by function and approval.                                               |\n| Who decides                      | RA 10121 places recovery under the local DRRMC; the NEDA guide sets the CDRRMC as the body that decides a city PDNA and the Sanggunian as the body that approves the local program. | Decision bodies are named in each SOP control block, separately from the operational owner.                                              |\n| Build Back Better                | A9.2 scores 2; C1.4 (permit review) 2; C2.1 (Building Official assessment) 1.                                                                                                       | SOP 5-05 screens every recovery project for hazard, siting, design standard and maintenance before it is funded.                         |\n| People, not only structures      | A9.4 scores 2; no referral pathways or MOAs.                                                                                                                                        | SOP 5-06 sets one referral pathway across CSWDO, CHO, PESO, City Agriculture and the housing office.                                     |\n| Track to completion              | A9.3 scores 1; no recovery monitoring matrix.                                                                                                                                       | SOP 5-07 and form REC-F06 track every recovery PPA to closeout; evaluation feeds Chapter 7.                                              |\n| Privacy                          | MODE stores no names of evacuees, patients or informants (RA 10173).                                                                                                                | Beneficiary lists stay in the restricted records of the office that serves the person; MODE and public reports hold aggregates only.     |\n\n## 1.3 The Recovery Continuum\n\nRecovery overlaps with response. Early recovery starts while relief is still being delivered; rehabilitation and reconstruction continue long after the EOC has closed. Figure 5.2 places the phases, the Chapter 4 handover and the eight SOPs of this chapter on one line.\n\n**Figure 5.2**\n\n*The Recovery Continuum From the Chapter 4 Handover to Closeout*\n\n![Figure 2: 1.3 The Recovery Continuum](/library/chp5v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Phase durations follow the NEDA (2020) guide: short-term PPAs within a year and medium-term PPAs within the next three to four years; the actual timeframes are set by the planning committee for each event. Prepared for this chapter.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets the City''s minimum arrangements for planning recovery before a disaster, assessing damage, losses and needs after it, preparing and approving a rehabilitation and recovery program, delivering it to Build Back Better standards, and tracking it to completion. It does not create new legal powers. Decisions on plans, budgets, land use, relocation and procurement stay with the authorities named in law and in valid City issuances.\n\nThe intended institutional results are:\n\n-   A pre-disaster recovery framework, adopted by the CDRRMC and updated each year, that names roles, PDNA activation, temporary shelter, debris management and recovery fund sources before an event (A9.5).\n\n-   A designated and trained RDANA and PDNA capability, with a written decision route from rapid assessment to PDNA and to CDRRMC approval (A8.7, A9.1).\n\n-   A controlled handover from response to recovery in which every open item from the Chapter 4 transition register has a named recovery owner (SOP 4-09 to SOP 5-03).\n\n-   A rehabilitation and recovery program, prepared after each major event, approved through the proper route and containing build-back-better and resettlement provisions (A9.2).\n\n-   Build-back-better screening of recovery projects before funding, linked to the land use plan, the zoning ordinance and the Building Official''s assessments (C1.4, C2.1).\n\n-   One referral pathway for psychosocial, social protection, livelihood, agriculture and shelter services, with consent and privacy controls (A9.4).\n\n-   A recovery monitoring matrix that tracks damage, losses, livelihood and housing outputs to completion and reports them to the CDRRMC (A9.3).\n\n-   Transparent recovery communication with a feedback and grievance channel for affected people.\n\n-   Outcome evaluation and after-action learning that revise plans and standards (A10.6, Chapter 7).\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\nThis chapter applies to recovery from any disaster that affects the City, whatever the hazard: tropical cyclones, floods and flash floods, landslides, earthquakes, tsunami, fire and industrial or geothermal incidents. It applies to the CDRRMC and its proposed Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee, the CDRRMO, the City offices that deliver recovery services, the barangays and their BDRRMCs, and the partners that work through City coordination arrangements. It covers pre-disaster recovery planning, damage and needs assessment, program formulation and approval, delivery, monitoring, evaluation and closeout.\n\nThe lead council depends on the area affected. The City leads when the disaster is confined to Ormoc and affects two or more barangays; a BDRRMC handles a disaster confined to one barangay. Because Ormoc is an independent component city, when the same disaster affects Ormoc and the Province of Leyte or another independent city, the RDRRMC VIII leads the overall rehabilitation and recovery coordination (NEDA, 2020). In that case the City still prepares its own local program, which feeds the regional program.\n\n**Table 5.7**\n\n*Institutional Authority Retained and Chapter 5 Interfaces*\n\n| **Institution / actor**                                  | **Authority and accountability retained**                                          | **Recovery interface under Chapter 5**                                                                                                                                                                         |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City Mayor**                                           | Executive authority under the Local Government Code and valid City issuances.      | Oversees the overall recovery effort with the support of the CDRRMO Head; directs City offices; submits budget measures and requests for assistance.                                                           |\n| **Sangguniang Panlungsod**                               | Legislative authority, including appropriations and local legislation.             | Approves the local rehabilitation and recovery program and the related appropriations and ordinances (NEDA, 2020, citing the Local Government Code).                                                           |\n| **CDRRMC**                                               | Statutory policy, coordination and oversight body under RA 10121.                  | Adopts the pre-disaster recovery framework; decides whether a City PDNA is conducted; approves the PDNA; prepares the rehabilitation and recovery program; monitors implementation.                            |\n| **Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee (proposed)**     | None of its own; a committee of the CDRRMC.                                        | Coordinates sector sub-committees, consolidates PPAs, tracks progress and resolves implementation issues. Proposed technical lead: CPDO, the recovery thematic focal office of the CDRRMP TWG (EO 2022-00020). |\n| **CDRRMO Head and CDRRMO**                               | Permanent technical office and secretariat of the CDRRMC.                          | Assists the Mayor in overseeing rehabilitation; leads the PDNA; keeps the recovery baseline and the monitoring matrix; runs SOPs 5-01, 5-02, 5-03 and 5-07.                                                    |\n| **CPDO**                                                 | Development planning, CLUP and investment programming.                             | Technical lead for program formulation, land use framework and integration into the CDP, AIP and CLUP.                                                                                                         |\n| **City Engineering Office / Building Official**          | Infrastructure delivery; building permits and structural safety under PD 1096.     | Infrastructure sub-committee lead; structural assessments; build-back-better design review (SOP 5-05).                                                                                                         |\n| **CSWDO**                                                | Social welfare, protection and camp coordination (JMC No. 2 s. 2021).              | Social services sub-committee lead; beneficiary registration; social protection, emergency shelter assistance and protection referrals.                                                                        |\n| **City Health Office**                                   | Public health and mental health services (RA 11036).                               | Health and MHPSS recovery; referrals; health facility restoration needs.                                                                                                                                       |\n| **PESO / BPLO / City Agriculture Office**                | Employment facilitation; business permits and MSME support; agricultural services. | Livelihood and agriculture sub-committees; emergency employment, MSME loan program, crop insurance claims and inputs.                                                                                          |\n| **City Budget Office, Treasurer, Accountant, BAC**       | Budget, treasury, accounting and procurement controls.                             | Resource mobilisation sub-committee; all financing and procurement steps follow Chapter 6.                                                                                                                     |\n| **Barangays / BDRRMCs**                                  | Barangay governance and local DRRM.                                                | Report local needs; help validate damage data; take part in planning and monitoring; lead recovery confined to one barangay.                                                                                   |\n| **OCD VIII, DEPDev VIII, RDRRMC VIII and line agencies** | National and regional mandates and funds.                                          | Assist the PDNA on City request; review the PDNA and program when NDRRMF funding is sought; lead regional recovery coordination when more than one LGU of the required kind is affected.                       |\n| **Private sector, CSOs, academe, development partners**  | Their own resources, contracts and organisational rules.                           | Contribute through pre-agreed MOAs and through City coordination channels (Chapter 8); independent monitoring where agreed.                                                                                    |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The decision and approval roles above follow the NEDA Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (2020), which is national guidance, not statute. The City Legal Office shall confirm the approval route for the local program, the role of the CDRRMC and the Sanggunian, and whether a Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee should be created by EO, by CDRRMC resolution or within the CDRRMP TWG under EO 2022-00020. NEDA has been reorganised as the Department of Economy, Planning, and Development (DEPDev) by RA No. 12145 (2025); references in the guide to NEDA are read as DEPDev.\n\nThree boundaries apply throughout. First, recovery begins inside response but does not take over response: life-safety actions stay under the Incident Commander and the EOC until the handover in SOP 4-09. Second, this chapter does not create land use restrictions, no-build zones, relocation orders or expenditure authority; these require the instruments named in Chapter 1 and Chapter 6. Third, Part C offices (CPDO, City Engineer, CENRO, utilities) keep ownership of their interface indicators; the CDRRMO coordinates and reports.\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nThe provisions of this chapter shall be read with the legal and policy hierarchy established in Chapter 1. Table 5.8 lists the principal anchors. The City Legal Office, OCD Region VIII and DEPDev Region VIII shall verify current texts and local approval routes before adoption.\n\n**Table 5.8**\n\n*Principal Legal, Policy and Operational Instruments for Recovery*\n\n| **Instrument / guidance**                                                                                                      | **Relevance to recovery**                                                                                                                                      | **Application in this chapter**                                                                            |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA No. 10121 (2010) and its IRR                                                                                                | Rehabilitation and recovery is a thematic area of the DRRM system; local DRRMCs and offices; LDRRMF including the Quick Response Fund for relief and recovery. | Basis of the CDRRMC and CDRRMO roles; indicator A9.2 cites Sec. 12(c)(11). Funding rules are in Chapter 6. |\n| RA No. 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                                                                                      | Local executive and legislative powers; local development planning and appropriations.                                                                         | Sanggunian approval of the program and appropriations.                                                     |\n| NDRRM Framework and NDRRMP 2020–2030                                                                                           | Rehabilitation and recovery outcomes and the Build Back Better principle.                                                                                      | Program outcomes and indicators; A9.2 and A9.5 cite the recovery priority area.                            |\n| Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020)                                                               | Recovery framework; pre-disaster activities; PDNA and RaDE; program contents; coordination structure; financing; communications; monitoring and evaluation.    | Primary guidance for every section and SOP of this chapter.                                                |\n| RA No. 12145 (2025), Economy, Planning and Development Act                                                                     | Reorganised NEDA into DEPDev.                                                                                                                                  | References to NEDA in national guidance are read as DEPDev.                                                |\n| OCD PDNA Guidance Notes; NDRRMC RDANA guidelines                                                                               | PDNA methodology, timeframes and team composition; RDANA triggers and forms.                                                                                   | SOP 5-02. Obtain the current editions from OCD VIII before the team is trained.                            |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030 (Priority 4; Targets C and D); UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard, Essential 10                    | Build Back Better; economic loss and critical infrastructure damage; expediting recovery.                                                                      | Good-practice benchmark for A9.3 and A9.5; data fields of the monitoring matrix.                           |\n| DSWD–DILG–DOH–DepEd JMC No. 2, s. 2021                                                                                         | Camp coordination and camp management; IDP protection, return and durable solutions.                                                                           | Closure of evacuation centres and transition of displaced families (SOP 5-03, 5-06).                       |\n| RA No. 11036 (Mental Health Act); RA No. 9710; RA No. 7277 as amended; RA No. 9994; RA No. 10821; RA No. 8371                  | Mental health and psychosocial support; gender, disability, older persons, children and indigenous peoples.                                                    | Inclusion safeguards and the referral pathway (sections 10–11, SOP 5-06).                                  |\n| RA No. 7279 (Urban Development and Housing Act) and DHSUD issuances                                                            | Socialised housing; conditions and due process for relocation and resettlement.                                                                                | Resettlement safeguards (section 9.3). Relocation is a last resort.                                        |\n| PD No. 1096 (National Building Code) and the structural code in force                                                          | Building permits and structural standards.                                                                                                                     | Build-back-better screening (SOP 5-05).                                                                    |\n| RA No. 12076 (Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act)                                                                                        | Standards for permanent evacuation centres.                                                                                                                    | Rebuilding or retrofitting evacuation centres.                                                             |\n| DHSUD/HLURB Supplemental Guidelines on Mainstreaming CC and DRR in the CLUP                                                    | Climate and disaster risk assessment; risk-sensitive land use.                                                                                                 | Post-disaster land use framework (section 9.2).                                                            |\n| RA No. 656 (Property Insurance Law); PCIC charter (PD 1467 as amended)                                                         | Insurance of government property through GSIS; crop and livestock insurance.                                                                                   | Recovery financing options (section 6.6), detailed in Chapter 6.                                           |\n| RA No. 12009 (New Government Procurement Act) and its IRR                                                                      | Procurement, including emergency and negotiated procurement.                                                                                                   | All recovery procurement follows Chapter 6.                                                                |\n| RA No. 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                                                                      | Personal and sensitive personal information of beneficiaries.                                                                                                  | Data minimisation and restricted beneficiary records (section 13.4, SOP 5-06).                             |\n| Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032; CDP 2024–2030; CLUP 2017–2027 and Zoning Ordinance; LCCAP 2019–2030; City Shelter Plan | Local recovery PPAs, development priorities, land use and hazard zoning.                                                                                       | Source of local PPAs (Table 5.5) and of the land use framework.                                            |\n| EO No. 2022-00020 (CDRRMP Technical Working Group); EO No. 2024-032A (CDRRMC)                                                  | CPDO named as the thematic focal office for recovery in the CDRRMP TWG; CDRRMC composition and functions.                                                      | Proposed technical lead for the Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee.                                     |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The NEDA guide describes national and regional practice; several statements (for example, that the NDRRMF covers rehabilitation of disasters in the last two years and is open to an LGU only when its LDRRMF is insufficient) reflect the rules at the time of publication. The CDRRMO shall confirm current NDRRMF access rules with OCD VIII and DBM before any proposal is prepared. The DSWD administrative orders on cash-for-work and emergency shelter assistance cited in the guide may have been superseded; CSWDO shall confirm the current issuances.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles for Recovery\n\n**Table 5.9**\n\n*Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles Applied to Recovery*\n\n| **Principle**                          | **Required application**                                                                                                                      | **Quality control**                                                                                     |\n|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Recovery starts before the event**   | Baseline data, scenarios, committee roles, scalable PPAs and financing options are prepared in normal times.                                  | Framework adopted and reviewed each year; recovery annex in every contingency plan.                     |\n| **Build Back Better**                  | Reconstruction raises resilience standards and includes climate adaptation, not only restoration.                                             | Every funded project passes BBB screening (REC-F07) with a recorded decision.                           |\n| **Evidence before money**              | Needs and costs come from the PDNA (or a RaDE when the PDNA is delayed); targets come from the PDNA.                                          | Each PPA cites its PDNA line or justification; no unsourced figures.                                    |\n| **People-centred and inclusive**       | Needs of women, children, older persons, persons with disability, indigenous peoples and the poorest households are identified and addressed. | Sex-, age- and disability-disaggregated aggregates; inclusion check in BBB screening; feedback channel. |\n| **Risk-informed land use**             | Rebuilding follows the CLUP, the Zoning Ordinance and current hazard maps.                                                                    | Siting check with CPDO and the Zoning Administrator before approval.                                    |\n| **One recovery picture**               | One monitoring matrix for all PPAs regardless of fund source.                                                                                 | Monthly consolidation; reconciliation with budget and procurement records.                              |\n| **PDCA / TQM**                         | Plan, deliver, check outputs and outcomes, correct.                                                                                           | Progress reports, problem-solving sessions, field validation and outcome evaluation.                    |\n| **Local ownership and accountability** | The City leads; partners align with the City program.                                                                                         | Partner commitments recorded in the matrix; public progress reports.                                    |\n| **Privacy by design**                  | Only the data needed to serve a person is collected, and only by the office that serves them.                                                 | No names in MODE or in general reports; restricted records with access logs.                            |\n\n# 6. Pre-Disaster Recovery Planning\n\nMost of the work that makes recovery fast is done before the disaster. The NEDA guide lists six pre-disaster activities for local DRRMCs: build the recovery database, prepare science-based scenarios, identify critical areas and populations, set up the recovery organisation, list PPAs that can be scaled up or reprogrammed, and arrange recovery financing (NEDA, 2020). This section assigns each one to an owner. SOP 5-01 turns them into an annual cycle, and Annex 5-C gives the outline of the resulting framework document.\n\n## 6.1 Recovery Baseline Database\n\nThe CDRRMO, as CDRRMC secretariat, keeps the recovery baseline. It reuses the Chapter 3 risk-information base and adds the data that recovery planning needs: population disaggregated by sex, age and disability at barangay level; housing and settlement data; livelihoods, farms and fisheries; MSMEs; critical facilities and lifelines; and social services. Annex 5-E lists the minimum data, adapted from Annex A of the NEDA guide. The barangay unit is the PSGC barangay (85 barangays as of 31 July 2025), pending the master-list reconciliation recorded in Chapter 3.\n\n-   Keep the baseline as aggregates by barangay. Household lists stay with the office that owns them (for example, CBMS with CPDO and beneficiary lists with CSWDO).\n\n-   Record the source and date of every dataset. A blank value means "not reported", never zero.\n\n-   Update the baseline at least once a year as part of SOP 5-01, and after every event that triggers a PDNA.\n\n## 6.2 Recovery Scenarios\n\nRecovery scenarios describe what a disaster of a given size would do to people, buildings, lifelines and livelihoods. They are taken from the CDRA and the contingency-plan scenarios of Chapter 3, so that the same most-probable and most-severe scenarios drive preparedness, response and recovery (C1.1). For each priority hazard the framework records the likely number of damaged houses, interrupted lifelines, affected farms and businesses, and the recovery needs these imply. Scenarios are prepared by the City with technical support from PHIVOLCS, PAGASA, MGB and NAMRIA; local and indigenous knowledge may be used.\n\n## 6.3 Critical Areas, Assets and Populations\n\nFrom the scenarios the CDRRMO and CPDO identify, before any event, the areas, assets and population groups whose recovery comes first: lifeline roads and bridges named in the CDRA, water and power facilities, the port, hospitals, schools used as evacuation centres, settlements in high-hazard zones, and barangays with high poverty incidence. The list sets the order of the PDNA field work and of the first PPAs.\n\n## 6.4 Pre-agreed Recovery Organisation\n\nThe recovery organisation is agreed in normal times and activated when a program is needed. The NEDA guide recommends a planning committee with sector sub-committees that may be replicated at local level. Table 5.10 adapts that structure to City offices. It is a proposal for validation and adoption by the CDRRMC.\n\n**Table 5.10**\n\n*Proposed Rehabilitation and Recovery Sub-committees of the CDRRMC*\n\n| **Sub-committee**                     | **Proposed lead office**                                | **Members (as relevant) and responsibilities**                                                                                                                                                       |\n|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Infrastructure**                    | City Engineering Office                                 | Building Official, CPDO, utilities (LEYECO V, water district, telecoms), DPWH district office. Repair and reconstruction of roads, bridges, public buildings and lifelines to resilient standards.   |\n| **Shelter and settlement**            | City housing office (OCHO as named in the 10-Year Plan) | CPDO, City Legal Office, CSWDO, City Engineering, NHA, Pag-IBIG, SHFC. Repair and rebuilding of houses; transitional and permanent housing; resettlement as a last resort.                           |\n| **Social services**                   | CSWDO                                                   | CHO, DepEd Schools Division, Population Office. Continued relief to the most vulnerable, psychosocial support, social protection, restoration of social service facilities and education continuity. |\n| **Livelihood and business**           | PESO, with BPLO                                         | City Cooperative or Economic Enterprise office, DTI, DOLE, TESDA, Chamber of Commerce. Emergency employment, restoration of income, MSME recovery and access to finance.                             |\n| **Agriculture and fisheries**         | City Agriculture Office                                 | City Veterinary Office, DA, BFAR, PCIC, NIA, Visayas State University. Farm and fishing inputs, crop insurance claims, buffer seed stocks, irrigation repair.                                        |\n| **Environment and debris**            | ENRO / CENRO                                            | City Engineering, General Services, DENR. Debris and post-disaster waste, ecosystem rehabilitation, watershed and mangrove restoration (C3).                                                         |\n| **Resource mobilisation and support** | City Budget Office, with the City Treasurer             | City Accountant, BAC, CDRRMO, City Legal Office. Budget review, fund sources, procurement, audit and partner coordination (Chapter 6).                                                               |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Box 7 and Part 3 of the NEDA (2020) guide, which lists infrastructure, shelter, social services, livelihood, agriculture and resource mobilisation sub-committees and allows others to be added. The environment and debris sub-committee is added for Ormoc because of the watershed and debris issues recorded in the CDRA and the 10-Year Plan. Lead offices are proposals.\n\n## 6.5 Scalable and Reprogrammable PPAs\n\nEach City office keeps a short list of its regular PPAs that can be scaled up or redirected to affected barangays after a disaster, with outputs, coverage, unit costs and the approval needed to realign them. Examples include the cash-for-work program, social pension and assistance programs, health programs, agricultural input programs and routine road and drainage maintenance. Regular programs are the fastest source of early recovery support because their beneficiary systems and budgets already exist (NEDA, 2020). The lists are consolidated in the framework and updated each year.\n\n## 6.6 Recovery Financing Options\n\nFinancing modalities are identified before the event so that the City does not wait for a budget cycle to start recovery. The detailed rules, ceilings and controls are in Chapter 6; Table 5.11 shows the options the framework must cover.\n\n**Table 5.11**\n\n*Recovery Financing Options to Be Identified in the Pre-disaster Framework*\n\n| **Source**                                                   | **Use in recovery**                                                                    | **Condition or control (see Chapter 6)**                                                   |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **LDRRMF 70% component**                                     | Rehabilitation and recovery PPAs programmed in the LDRRMF Investment Plan.             | Must be in the LDRRMFIP and AIP; utilisation reporting.                                    |\n| **Quick Response Fund (30%)**                                | Standby fund for relief and recovery so that living conditions are normalised quickly. | Use under the conditions set in Chapter 6, including the State of Calamity requirement.    |\n| **Special Trust Fund**                                       | Unexpended LDRRMF available for DRRM PPAs, including recovery.                         | Five-year use period; ageing ledger in Chapter 6.                                          |\n| **Realigned regular budgets of City offices**                | Scaled-up regular programs (section 6.5).                                              | Realignment authority and Sanggunian action where required.                                |\n| **Supplemental budget**                                      | Large financing gaps after the budget year has started.                                | Sanggunian appropriation.                                                                  |\n| **NDRRMF and national programs**                             | Rehabilitation PPAs that the LDRRMF cannot cover.                                      | Proposal through RDRRMC VIII to the NDRRMC; confirm current access rules (section 4 note). |\n| **GSIS property insurance (RA 656)**                         | Rebuilding of insured City buildings and assets.                                       | Assets must be insured before the event; claims filed promptly.                            |\n| **PCIC crop, livestock and fisheries insurance**             | Recovery of farm and fisheries investment.                                             | Farmer enrolment before the event; City Agriculture supports claims.                       |\n| **Development partners, private sector, CSOs and donations** | Co-financing or direct implementation of PPAs.                                         | Recorded in the City program; donations rules of Chapter 6.                                |\n\n*Note.* Sources are drawn from Part 4 and Table 1 of the NEDA (2020) guide and from the LDRRMF rules summarised in the MODE Platform Coherence Analysis (12 November 2026). Amounts, ceilings and section citations are governed by Chapter 6, including the open verification of the COA Circular 2012-002 section on trust-fund transfers.\n\n## 6.7 The Pre-disaster Recovery Framework\n\nThe six products above are brought together in one short document: the Ormoc Pre-disaster Recovery Framework. It is adopted by the CDRRMC, attached as the recovery annex of each hazard-specific contingency plan (Chapter 3, SOP 3-06), and reviewed every year. The IAT recommends the OCD and World Bank "Ready to Rebuild" workbook as the working tool (A9.5); the 10-Year Plan programs PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training for this purpose. Annex 5-C gives the framework outline.\n\n# 7. Damage, Loss and Needs Assessment\n\nRecovery decisions depend on knowing what was damaged, what was lost and what people need. Three assessments are used, at different times and for different purposes (Table 5.12). The RDANA belongs to response (Chapter 4) and is carried out by the A8.7 team. The PDNA belongs to recovery and is the main source for the rehabilitation and recovery program.\n\n**Table 5.12**\n\n*Comparison of Damage and Needs Assessments Used by the City*\n\n| **Feature**    | **RDANA**                                                                        | **RaDE**                                                                                  | **PDNA**                                                                                 |\n|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Purpose**    | Initial damage and immediate relief and response needs.                          | Quick desk estimate of damage, losses and recovery needs while a PDNA is being completed. | Detailed sectoral and cross-sectoral damage, losses, impacts and recovery needs.         |\n| **When**       | In the first days, during response.                                              | Optional; for large events when the PDNA will take long.                                  | After response stabilises; can run alongside the end of response.                        |\n| **Local lead** | CDRRMO RDANA team (A8.7).                                                        | CDRRMO with CPDO.                                                                         | CDRRMO, with OCD VIII support on City request.                                           |\n| **Method**     | Desk review and field validation; ASEAN ERAT Initial Needs Assessment Checklist. | Desk exercise on available data.                                                          | Desk review and field validation; UN-ECLAC damage and loss method; human recovery needs. |\n| **Coverage**   | Mostly public-sector damage and immediate needs.                                 | Public and private damage and losses; overall economic effect.                            | Public and private damage and losses; economic impact; recovery needs.                   |\n| **Feeds**      | SitReps, response decisions (Chapter 4).                                         | Early resource mobilisation and budget review.                                            | Rehabilitation and recovery program (section 8).                                         |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Table 2 of the NEDA (2020) guide; at LGU level the lead is the LDRRMO. The guide cites NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 25, 2014 for RDANA triggers; the CDRRMO shall confirm the current RDANA issuance with OCD VIII.\n\n## 7.1 Deciding to Conduct a City PDNA\n\nThe CDRRMC decides whether the City conducts a PDNA when a disaster affects two or more barangays. The decision is based on the RDANA results and the extent of damage, and the usual triggers are a local declaration of a State of Calamity or a need for financial assistance from the national government (NEDA, 2020). When the CDRRMC decides not to conduct a PDNA, it records why and how recovery needs will be met (for example, through the regular programs of section 6.5). Figure 5.3 shows the decision and approval route.\n\n**Figure 5.3**\n\n*PDNA Decision, Approval and Program Route for a City-level Disaster*\n\n![Figure 3: 7.1 Deciding to Conduct a City PDNA](/library/chp5v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from Part 2 of the NEDA (2020) guide. Shaded boxes are decision bodies. The route through RDRRMC VIII applies only when national funding is requested. The approval route remains subject to City Legal Office confirmation.\n\n## 7.2 PDNA Team and Readiness\n\nThe CDRRMO leads the PDNA. The team is organised by sector, drawing on the offices that lead the recovery sub-committees (Table 5.10), with a CDRRMO coordinator and a data manager. The 10-Year Plan programs five PDNA technical positions and PDNA training for CDRRMC and BDRRMC members; until those positions are filled, the team is designated by office order from existing staff and trained with OCD VIII. Team composition, methods and timeframes follow the current OCD PDNA Guidance Notes. Templates are kept ready in the Library so that the team does not design forms after the event.\n\n## 7.3 Assessment Data Standards\n\n-   Use the PSGC barangay as the reporting unit and the pre-disaster baseline (section 6.1) as the reference.\n\n-   Keep figures as aggregates. Damage to individual houses may be recorded by the Building Official and CSWDO in their restricted records, but reports carry counts.\n\n-   Disaggregate affected population by sex, age and disability wherever the source allows.\n\n-   Separate confirmed figures from figures for validation, as in the Chapter 4 SitRep rules, and record the source of each figure.\n\n-   Record assumptions and unit costs used to estimate damage and losses, so that the figures can be reviewed and reproduced.\n\n# 8. Rehabilitation and Recovery Program\n\n## 8.1 Trigger and Timeframe\n\nThe CDRRMC prepares a local rehabilitation and recovery program for a disaster confined to the City when a State of Calamity is declared or national financial assistance is needed. It may also prepare one for a larger disaster, as the City''s input to the regional program (NEDA, 2020). Program preparation may start while response is still under way. For past typhoons, local and regional programs took an average of three months to prepare, excluding approval; this is a planning benchmark, not a legal deadline. Priority PPAs for the first year may be approved as an interim document before the full program, so that early recovery is not held up by the approval process.\n\n## 8.2 Program Contents\n\n**Table 5.13**\n\n*Minimum Contents of the Ormoc Rehabilitation and Recovery Program*\n\n| **Section**                                 | **Content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Background and affected areas**           | Location, topography, climate, natural resources, pre-disaster economy, social and demographic data from the recovery baseline.                                                                                                          |\n| **Description of the hazard event**         | Hazard and immediate effects, from SitReps and the products of PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and MGB.                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Assessment of effects**                   | Physical assessment (damage to infrastructure, changes to landscape) and socio-economic assessment (damage and losses by sector, affected, displaced, dead, missing and injured persons, situation of vulnerable groups), from the PDNA. |\n| **Recovery framework**                      | Goal, overall outcome, sector outcomes and cross-cutting concerns (Figure 5.4).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Objectives and strategies**               | Achievable objectives with a timeframe; sector strategies consistent with Build Back Better.                                                                                                                                             |\n| **Targets**                                 | Taken from the PDNA, disaggregated by year and by barangay.                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **Land use framework**                      | Locations for infrastructure, settlements and economic activities, based on the CLUP, Zoning Ordinance, shelter plan and updated hazard information (section 9.2).                                                                       |\n| **Phasing and implementation arrangements** | Short-term (within a year) and medium-term (three to four years) PPAs; lead and support offices.                                                                                                                                         |\n| **Financing and investment requirements**   | Investment requirement by source of fund and by sector; detailed PPA list (form REC-F04).                                                                                                                                                |\n| **Monitoring and evaluation**               | Monitoring unit, reporting arrangements and the monitoring matrix (section 13).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Communication strategy**                  | Principles, core messages, spokesperson, materials and channels (section 12).                                                                                                                                                            |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Part 2, Section B of the NEDA (2020) guide.\n\n**Figure 5.4**\n\n*Recovery Framework for an Ormoc Rehabilitation and Recovery Program*\n\n![Figure 4: 8.2 Program Contents](/library/chp5v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Adapted from the General Framework for Post-Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery (NEDA, 2020, Figure 3), which the Ormoc DRRM 10-Year Plan also uses as the base of its Framework for Local Resilience. Sector outcomes and cross-cutting concerns are tailored for each event; data privacy is added for Ormoc.\n\n## 8.3 Phasing of PPAs\n\nShort-term PPAs, implemented within a year, meet basic needs and restore social services so that families can return to normal life. Medium-term PPAs, implemented over the following three to four years, continue those programs and add measures that reduce future risk. Some reconstruction may run longer. In the 10-Year Plan, livelihood, social services and agriculture outcomes are targeted in the short term, while housing and infrastructure need phasing for studies, land acquisition and social preparation (City Government of Ormoc, 2023).\n\n## 8.4 Approval and Updating\n\nThe CDRRMC prepares and endorses the program; the Sangguniang Panlungsod approves it and the related appropriations. When funding from the national government is requested, the CDRRMC submits the Sanggunian-approved program through the RDRRMC VIII to the NDRRMC, and the RDRRMC reviews and validates the City PDNA (NEDA, 2020). The program includes an investment program that is revised as implementation progresses, based on the physical and financial accomplishment reports of SOP 5-07.\n\n# 9. Build Back Better\n\nBuild Back Better is applied as a decision rule, not a slogan. Every recovery project that rebuilds or repairs a structure, a lifeline, a settlement or an ecosystem is screened before it is funded (SOP 5-05, form REC-F07). The screen asks whether the project reduces risk compared with what was there before, and it records the decision and its reasons.\n\n**Table 5.14**\n\n*Build-Back-Better Criteria by Recovery Sector*\n\n| **Sector**                                  | **Build-back-better criterion**                                                                                                                                              | **Evidence and reviewer**                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Public buildings and evacuation centres** | Rebuilt or retrofitted to the structural code in force and the Building Official''s assessment; evacuation centres to RA 12076 standards; access for persons with disability. | Structural assessment and design review by the Building Official (C2.1). |\n| **Roads, bridges and drainage**             | Design accounts for the observed flood levels and hazard maps; alternate routes and critical segments identified in the CDRA are prioritised.                                | City Engineering and DPWH design review (C2.3).                          |\n| **Lifelines (power, water, telecoms)**      | Restoration priorities and redundancy agreed with providers; critical facilities first.                                                                                      | Restoration-priority arrangements with utilities (C2.2).                 |\n| **Housing and settlements**                 | Rebuilding only where the CLUP and Zoning Ordinance allow; floor elevation, setbacks and materials suited to the hazard; relocation only as a last resort with due process.  | CPDO, Zoning Administrator and housing office siting check (C1.4).       |\n| **Livelihoods**                             | Diversified or less hazard-exposed income; insurance or savings where available.                                                                                             | PESO, BPLO and City Agriculture; enrolment in PCIC or microinsurance.    |\n| **Ecosystems**                              | Watershed, forest and mangrove restoration that reduces hazard; safe debris and waste disposal.                                                                              | ENRO/CENRO and DENR (C3.1, C3.3).                                        |\n| **Social services**                         | Services restored and upgraded with inclusion standards; child-protection limits on schools used as shelters (RA 10821).                                                     | CSWDO, CHO, DepEd.                                                       |\n\n## 9.1 What Build Back Better Does Not Authorise\n\nThis Manual does not declare no-build zones, change land use classifications, order relocation or set design thresholds. Those require an ordinance, a valid executive issuance, a building permit decision or a technical standard issued by the competent body. The screen in SOP 5-05 records whether such an instrument exists and, if it does not, refers the issue to the authority that can issue it. One such instrument already exists: City Ordinance No. 37, s. 1993 declares parts of the Alegria–Punta shoreline and the Anilao and Malbasag river banks as high-risk areas where habitation, construction and building permits are prohibited (Chapter 1, section 4.5). Its boundaries must be reconciled with current hazard maps before the screen relies on it.\n\n## 9.2 Post-disaster Land Use Framework\n\nFor a disaster with major physical impact, the CPDO prepares a land use framework for the program that identifies where infrastructure, settlements and economic activities will be rebuilt, based on the CLUP, the Zoning Ordinance, the shelter plan and updated risk assessments. Where the disaster changes the landscape (for example, new landslide scars or river course changes), the framework proposes the changes needed in the CLUP or Zoning Ordinance for Sanggunian action. Where there is no significant physical change, the framework is used to enforce the existing land use policies (NEDA, 2020).\n\n## 9.3 Resettlement Safeguards\n\n-   Relocation is a last resort, after in-place rebuilding with risk reduction has been considered and found unsafe.\n\n-   Relocation follows RA 7279 and the applicable DHSUD rules, including consultation, adequate notice and due process.\n\n-   Resettlement sites include livelihood, social services, infrastructure and utilities, and are themselves screened for hazard.\n\n-   Affected families, including women, older persons and persons with disability, take part in site selection and design.\n\n-   The City records each decision, its legal basis and the consultation held; individual household records stay with the housing office and CSWDO.\n\n# 10. Sector Recovery Services\n\nRecovery services are delivered by the offices that hold the mandate. The CDRRMO coordinates and tracks; it does not take over service delivery. Table 5.15 sets the minimum service content for each sector.\n\n**Table 5.15**\n\n*Minimum Recovery Service Content by Sector*\n\n| **Sector**                         | **Lead office**                   | **Minimum service content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Shelter and housing**            | Housing office with CSWDO         | Emergency shelter assistance for repair or rebuilding in safe sites; transitional shelter where needed; permanent housing through the LGU-funded housing program and NHA, Pag-IBIG or SHFC; land acquisition for settlement sites as programmed in the 10-Year Plan. |\n| **Livelihood and employment**      | PESO with BPLO                    | Cash-for-work using the City''s existing program; emergency employment with DOLE; MSME recovery and the planned LGU-managed MSME loan program; skills training with TESDA.                                                                                            |\n| **Agriculture and fisheries**      | City Agriculture Office           | Inputs and replanting; buffer seed stocks (programmed); support for PCIC claims; repair of farm and fisheries infrastructure with DA, BFAR and NIA.                                                                                                                  |\n| **Social services and protection** | CSWDO                             | Continued assistance to the most vulnerable until they recover; social protection; protection case management; closure of evacuation centres and support for return or durable solutions under JMC No. 2 s. 2021.                                                    |\n| **Health and MHPSS**               | City Health Office                | Mental health and psychosocial support under RA 11036; restoration of health services and facilities; disease surveillance in affected areas.                                                                                                                        |\n| **Education**                      | DepEd Schools Division with CSWDO | Return of schools used as shelters; repair of classrooms; learning continuity.                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Infrastructure and lifelines**   | City Engineering with utilities   | Priority restoration list; repair and reconstruction to Build Back Better criteria (Table 5.14).                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Environment and debris**         | ENRO / CENRO                      | Debris clearing and post-disaster waste management (programmed in the 10-Year Plan); ecosystem rehabilitation.                                                                                                                                                       |\n\n## 10.1 Referral Pathway\n\nPeople rarely need only one service. A family that lost its house may also have lost its income and may need psychosocial support. The referral pathway in Figure 5.5 gives every entry point the same screening and referral form (REC-F05) and the same consent and privacy rules. SOP 5-06 sets the steps. Referral pathways and MOAs among CSWDO, CHO and PESO are the recommended action for A9.4.\n\n**Figure 5.5**\n\n*Recovery Referral Pathway Across City Service Offices*\n\n![Figure 5: 10.1 Referral Pathway](/library/chp5v10/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Personal details stay in the restricted record of the office that serves the person; the pathway shares only what the receiving office needs, with consent. BHW = barangay health worker; ESA = emergency shelter assistance; MHPSS = mental health and psychosocial support.\n\n# 11. Inclusion, Protection and Community Participation\n\nRecovery is hardest for those who were most vulnerable before the disaster: displaced families; orphaned, unaccompanied and separated children; older persons; persons with disability; single-parent households; indigenous peoples; and farmers, fisherfolk, farm workers and small traders who lose their income (NEDA, 2020). The program shall:\n\n-   Set priority beneficiaries using pre-disaster vulnerability, capacity to recover and access to services, and record the criteria publicly.\n\n-   Use sex-, age- and disability-disaggregated aggregates and gender-sensitive indicators in the monitoring matrix; the Harmonized Gender and Development Guidelines may be used to review PPAs.\n\n-   Respect culture, beliefs and cultural sites, and take account of the needs of indigenous peoples in restoring their assets.\n\n-   Consult affected communities, including women, youth, older persons and persons with disability, during assessment, planning, implementation and monitoring.\n\n-   Provide a feedback and grievance channel that is accessible and that does not depend on literacy or a mobile phone (SOP 5-08).\n\n-   Protect people from exploitation and abuse in the delivery of assistance, and refer protection cases to CSWDO.\n\n# 12. Recovery Communication and Accountability\n\nA recovery program builds trust only if people know what is planned, when it will happen and how to raise a problem. The CDRRMC designates a spokesperson and a communication focal person for recovery at the start of the program, and adopts a communication strategy that covers the principles, core messages, materials and channels below (NEDA, 2020).\n\n**Table 5.16**\n\n*Recovery Communication Strategy Components*\n\n| **Component**       | **Minimum content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Principles**      | One communication protocol for all offices; one spokesperson; manage expectations with proactive messages about delays and beneficiary selection; a feedback mechanism with responses; a regular release schedule with consistent data.                               |\n| **Core messages**   | Roles of government, communities and partners; sector priorities; priority PPAs and targets; governing policies; available funds; start and completion dates; implementation issues and how they are being resolved; how communities can take part; overall progress. |\n| **Materials**       | Plain-language notices in Cebuano and Waray where needed, accessible formats, barangay posters, radio scripts and progress snapshots.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Channels**        | Barangay assemblies, BDRRMC meetings, City public information channels, local radio, social media, and the feedback desk and hotline.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Snapshot report** | A short, regular summary of the main recovery issues and trends for the Mayor and CDRRMC, shared with City offices so that all work from the same figures.                                                                                                            |\n\nPublic reports use the same aggregate figures as the monitoring matrix. Corrections are published when a figure changes. Full disclosure of DRRM funds and their use follows Chapter 1 and Chapter 6.\n\n# 13. Recovery Monitoring, Tracking to Completion and Evaluation\n\n## 13.1 Monitoring Unit\n\nThe CDRRMC designates the unit responsible for monitoring the program before implementation starts, so that monitoring is not left out (NEDA, 2020). This chapter proposes the CDRRMO Research and Planning function, working with the CPDO monitoring staff, as the recovery monitoring unit. Its responsibilities are to set key performance indicators; orient implementing offices on the monitoring tool; consolidate their reports; keep the project database up to date; take up implementation issues with the offices concerned; prepare periodic status reports to the CDRRMC; and validate reports through field visits to key projects. If a task force is created for a large event, the transfer of the monitoring function back to the permanent unit is agreed at the start.\n\n## 13.2 Recovery Monitoring Matrix\n\nAll recovery PPAs, whatever their fund source or implementing office, are tracked in one recovery monitoring matrix (form REC-F06). This closes the A9.3 gap and is the recommended action for that indicator ("add a recovery monitoring matrix to the event database"). Annex 5-D lists the minimum fields.\n\n## 13.3 Reporting and Problem Solving\n\n-   Each implementing office names a monitoring focal person and reports physical and financial accomplishment against targets at the frequency set by the CDRRMC. The NEDA guide requires at least an annual report; this chapter proposes monthly reports during the first year and quarterly reports afterwards, for CDRRMC decision.\n\n-   Issues that block implementation are raised as they arise, not only in the formal report.\n\n-   A regular inter-office problem-solving meeting, with the monitoring unit as secretariat, resolves bottlenecks.\n\n-   The monitoring unit and office focal persons validate key and problem projects in the field.\n\n## 13.4 Data Protection in Recovery Records\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
